      "latency_ms": 1801.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 85,
      "completion_tokens": 766,
      "output": "**Water** is a simple chemical compound that is essential for life on Earth. Its basic characteristics are:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical formula** | **H₂O** – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular structure** | Bent (angular) shape with a bond angle of about 104.5°, giving the molecule a permanent dipole (partial negative charge on oxygen, partial positive on hydrogens). |\n| **Physical states** | Exists naturally as **solid (ice), liquid (water), and gas (water vapor/steam)**. Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing/melting) and 100 °C (boiling) at 1 atm pressure. |\n| **Key properties** | • **High specific heat** – can absorb a lot of heat without large temperature change, moderating climate.<br>• **High heat of vaporization** – requires much energy to evaporate, driving cooling processes (e.g., sweating).<br>• **Universal solvent** – polar nature dissolves many ionic and polar substances, making it the “solvent of life.”<br>• **Surface tension** – strong cohesion between molecules creates a “skin” on the surface, enabling phenomena like capillary action.<br>• **Density anomaly** – water is most dense at 4 °C; ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats. |\n| **Occurrence** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface (≈ 97 % of it is saltwater). It also exists in the atmosphere, soils, glaciers, and within living organisms (≈ 60 % of the human body by weight). |\n| **Biological role** | • **Medium for biochemical reactions** – enzymes, metabolism, and transport of nutrients occur in aqueous environments.<br>• **Structural component** – cells are ~70 % water; water maintains turgor pressure in plants and provides cushioning in animals.<br>• **Regulation of temperature** – through sweating, respiration, and evaporative cooling. |\n| **Industrial & everyday uses** | Drinking, agriculture, sanitation, power generation (hydropower, steam turbines), manufacturing (chemical synthesis, cooling), recreation, and many more. |\n| **Environmental importance** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff continuously redistribute water, shaping weather and ecosystems.<br>• **Climate regulation** – oceans store and transport heat around the globe.<br>• **Habitat** – freshwater lakes, rivers, and wetlands support diverse flora and fauna. |\n\n### Why Water Is Unique\n1. **Polarity** gives it strong hydrogen‑bonding capabilities, leading to its high boiling point, surface tension, and solvent power.\n2. **Hydrogen bonds** are transient (lasting picoseconds) but collectively give water its cohesive and adhesive properties.\n3. **Thermal stability** helps buffer temperature fluctuations in organisms and environments.\n4. **Anomalous density** ensures that ice forms on the surface of lakes and oceans, insulating the water below and protecting aquatic life in cold climates.\n\n### Summary\nWater (H₂O) is a polar, hydrogen‑bonding molecule that can exist as solid, liquid, or gas under Earth‑normal conditions. Its exceptional physical and chemical properties make it the medium of life, a key player in Earth’s climate system, and a versatile resource for countless natural and human processes."
